A||||5||jimig||The story so far...||||7||
Z||000000||jimig||10-27-2000||12:01 PM||||First off, I recently discovered this forum and I enjoy checking it several times a day.<P>A question for everyone; have any of you owned your Fiero since it was new?  Also, what kind of trouble have you gone through to keep it?<P>This is my story and I am sticking to it.  When the Fiero made it's debut in 1983, I was 19 years old and in my first of two years in a technical college in Northern Wisconsin.  It was love at first sight.  I knew that I could not afford one yet.  But by 1985 I was started on my career and I figured that I could afford a 2M6 but not a GT.  So I went to the dealer to check on one.  The salesman told me that I could lease a GT for about the same payment as buying a 2M6.  Being stupid about working deals, I said OK.  I will never lease again, but on with it.  They had a red GT on order and it could be mine if I wanted it.  Well, even though I wanted a black one, I said OK.<P>On June 11, 1985, three days before my 21st birthday, I took delivery of my 1985 Fiero GT.  The color is red/silver with the only options other than the GT package, rear spoiler, sunroof and air conditioning.  The window sticker price was $13,444.  I still have the window sticker.<P>Anyway, I still have the Fiero.  It has survived through marriage (I told my wife never to ask me to get rid of the Fiero or my 1968 Firebird 400 convertible) and 3 kids (maybe 4 someday).  It currently has 92,000 miles on it (no winters) and is completely original except for the front nose that was damaged in an accident, the rear wing that someone tried to rip off (I still have the original), one of the radiator hoses that got a hole in it where it was hitting the plastic inner fender and the front brakes that I just replaced.  I have gas mileage records going back to day one, every Fiero brochure that I can get my hands on, the MPC Fiero GT model, chassis manual and a few odds and ends.<P>Well this post is getting kind of long, so let me close by saying I LOVE THIS CAR.<P>------------------<BR>jimig<BR>1985 Fiero GT<BR>1968 Firebird 400 convertible<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-27-2000||12:50 PM||||I've had my 87 GT (loaded) since new (5 miles, now 82700).  Mostly routine maintenance work done to the car.  A/C repaired/replaced  ($800) a couple of years ago. Borla exhaust and cat replaced stock exhaust last year. Replaced distributor and ignition modual last year as well as clutch. Still have original rear brakes, orig suspension.  EGR valve replaced, repainted wing (faded), nose and rocker panels (chips). I'm on the second replacement battery and 3rd set of tires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-27-2000||01:19 PM||||My first Fiero (still have) was the 1986 Gold SE, V6 + 4-speed.  First and only new car I ever bought.  Never regretted buying it.<P>Routine maintenance consisted of DIY oil changes, about every 6 months.  Replaced OE clutch once; now it's ready for new one again.  Changed plugs twice or thrice; mostly just "because I suppose I ought to."<BR> <BR>Very tough car.  Rear-ended twice; wrecked LR suspension once.  Repaired to absolutely good as new condition each time.  <P>It's finally beginning showing signs of age.  Upholstery needs a little help; clutch is hanging in there, but will need attention soon (been saying that for years   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  Motor not quite as tight as it used to be, but darn nice for a 14-year old. Still get compliments on its looks and condition  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||10-27-2000||09:50 PM||||All of mine I bought about a year ago.<BR>Hey I see you live in Germantown, WI...cool...I live in Germantown, Tn! (suburb of Memphis)..oh well, thought that was interesting.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
